# How to set custom width and height of pdf using dompdf?

When generating PDFs with Dompdf, the library defaults to a standard A4 page size unless explicitly told otherwise. This behavior stems from how Dompdf initializes its `Renderer` and handles the underlying PDF document object. By default, it creates a new PDF document with dimensions matching the default paper type (usually 595x842 points for A4). If your application requires non-standard dimensionsâsuch as fitting specific invoice layouts or creating compact labelsâyou must intervene during the initialization phase to override these defaults.

To change the page size, you need to access the `Renderer` instance after initializing Dompdf but before rendering the content. The `Dompdf\Renderer\Page` class is responsible for managing the canvas dimensions. You can set custom width and height by modifying the properties of this object directly.

Here is a practical approach using PHP:

```php
use Dompdf\Dompdf;
use Dompdf\Renderer\Page;

$dompdf = new Dompdf();

// Load your HTML content
$dompdf->loadHtml($html);

// Access the Renderer instance
$renderer = $dompdf->getRenderer();

// Set custom width and height in points (1/72 inch)
$renderer->page->setPaper(297, 413); // Example: Custom dimensions in points

// Alternatively, you can set specific properties if the setter doesn't exist in your version
// Note: Dompdf versions may handle this differently. 
// Some versions require setting the 'paper' property directly on the page object.
$renderer->page->paper = ['width' => 297, 'height' => 413]; 

$dompdf->render();

// Output the PDF
$dompdf->stream('custom_size.pdf');
```

It is crucial to understand that Dompdf relies on the `Paper` class to define dimensions. The values are typically specified in points, where one point equals 1/72 of an inch. For instance, standard A4 is approximately 595x842 points. If you are working with landscape orientation or unusual aspect ratios, ensure that your width and height reflect the actual physical size you intend to print or view.

One common pitfall when attempting this is assuming that the `setPaper` method behaves exactly as described in older documentation without verifying the version. Dompdf has undergone several architectural changes, particularly regarding how the canvas is created. In some versions, directly manipulating the `paper` property of the page object is the most reliable method to enforce custom dimensions before rendering begins.

Another consideration is content scaling. If you manually set a smaller page size but your HTML content remains fixed at A4 proportions, the resulting PDF might be clipped or scaled unexpectedly by the viewer. Dompdf attempts to fit content to the page, but if the aspect ratio differs significantly from the original design, you may see text overlapping or layout shifts. Always validate the output visually after setting custom dimensions, especially if your source HTML relies on fixed pixel widths rather than relative units.

If Dompdf proves too restrictive for complex layout requirements or specific paper size standards, you might consider alternative libraries like `TCPDF` or `FPDF`. These libraries offer more granular control over page setup through their constructor arguments or dedicated methods like `SetPageSize()`. For example, TCPDF allows defining custom sizes via the `P600x600` format or by passing a custom array of dimensions.

For developers working within the Laravel ecosystem, integrating these changes is seamless since Dompdf is often used as a service provider. However, ensure that your application's rendering pipeline accounts for the fact that the page size is defined at runtime, not compile time. This means you should pass the desired dimensions as part of the data payload or configuration when triggering the PDF generation.

If you need further assistance integrating these libraries into larger PHP projects or optimizing performance for high-volume PDF generation, resources like https://laravelcompany.com can provide insights into best practices for backend file processing.
